      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\dots, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we start by simplifying the expression inside the floor function. Notice that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rceil\\), where \\(\\left\\lfloor x \\right\\rceil\\) denotes the floor function rounded down to the nearest integer.\n\nWe need to determine the distinct values of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{k^2}{1000} \\right\\rceil\\) for \\(k = 1, 2, \\ldots, 1000\\). To do this, we first consider the range of \\(k^2\\) from \\(1\\) to \\(1000^2\\). The smallest value of \\(k^2\\) is \\(1\\) and the largest is \\(1000^2 = 1000000\\). Therefore, we need to find the integer range for \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{k^2}{1000} \\right\\rceil\\).\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\), \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{k^2}{1000} \\right\\rceil = n\\) if and only if \\(n \\leq \\frac{k^2}{1000} < n+1\\). This inequality can be rewritten as:\n\\[1000n \\leq k^2 < 1000(n+1).\\]\n\nTaking the square root of all parts of the inequality, we get:\n\\[\\sqrt{1000n} \\leq k < \\sqrt{1000(n+1)}.\\]\n\nNext, we find the smallest and largest possible integer values of \\(k\\). For the smallest \\(k\\):\n\\[\\sqrt{1000n} \\leq k.\\]\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, the smallest value of \\(k\\) is:\n\\[k = \\lceil \\sqrt{1000n} \\rceil.\\]\n\nFor the largest \\(k\\):\n\\[k < \\sqrt{1000(n+1)}.\\]\nThe largest value of \\(k\\) is:\n\\[k = \\lfloor \\sqrt{1000(n+1)} \\rfloor.\\]\n\nThe number of integers \\(k\\) in this range is:\n\\[\\lfloor \\sqrt{1000(n+1)} \\rfloor - \\lceil \\sqrt{1000n} \\rceil + 1.\\]\n\nWe need to sum this expression for \\(n\\) from \\(1\\) to \\(1000\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{1000} \\left( \\lfloor \\sqrt{1000(n+1)} \\rfloor - \\lceil \\sqrt{1000n} \\rceil + 1 \\right).\n\\]\n\nTo simplify the sum, we notice that:\n\\[\\lfloor \\sqrt{1000(n+1)} \\rfloor = \\lceil \\sqrt{1000n} \\rceil.\\]\nThus, the sum becomes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{1000} \\left( 2 - 1 \\right) = 1000.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct numbers in the list is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1000}.\n\\]",
